Comment: "If the inserted element is null, then the last element of the list is also null; otherwise, the last element of the list is equal to the inserted element."

We need to use observer methods: after operation, we can call this.getLast() maybe? The class has method getLast() which returns the last element. Also we have addLast(e) called earlier, which inserts element at end. So spec: after calling offerLast(e), if e == null then this.getLast() == null else this.getLast().equals(e). Use \result? Not needed.

We need to express conditional: (e == null) => this.getLast() == null && (e != null) => this.getLast().equals(e). Use => syntax.

Thus assert: (e == null) => this.getLast() == null && (e != null) => this.getLast().equals(e);

But need parentheses due to => lower precedence than &&. So we can write: (e == null) => this.getLast() == null && (e != null) => this.getLast().equals(e);

But maybe need parentheses around each implication: (e == null) => (this.getLast() == null) && (e != null) => (this.getLast().equals(e));

But careful: && binds stronger than =>? Actually => has lower priority than &&, meaning && evaluated before =>? The rule: => has lower priority than && and ||, so need parentheses to ensure correct grouping. If we write A => B && C => D, it would parse as A => (B && C) => D? ambiguous. Better separate with parentheses:

(e == null) => (this.getLast() == null) && (e != null) => (this.getLast().equals(e))

But still ambiguous: due to left associativity? Safer to combine with && inside parentheses: ((e == null) => (this.getLast() == null)) && ((e != null) => (this.getLast().equals(e)))

Thus final assert:

assert ((e == null) => (this.getLast() == null)) && ((e != null) => (this.getLast().equals(e)));

